lokihouse Debian-specific notes
===============================

* ClickHouse is not packaged in Debian. Install it from ClickHouse's
  repository or point clickhouse.addr at a remote server.

* The service is not enabled on install. Edit /etc/lokihouse/lokihouse.yaml,
  then run: systemctl enable --now lokihouse
  The file lists every option with its default, commented out; uncomment
  and change what you need. A copy is in
  /usr/share/doc/lokihouse/examples/lokihouse.example.yaml, which dpkg does
  not touch, to diff against after an upgrade.

* The service runs as a dynamic user with state in /var/lib/lokihouse.
  Point ruler_storage.filesystem.dir at /var/lib/lokihouse/rules.

* Put the ClickHouse password in a systemd credential:
    systemd-creds encrypt --name=lokihouse.clickhouse-password - \
      /etc/credstore.encrypted/lokihouse.clickhouse-password
  then uncomment the password_file line in /etc/lokihouse/lokihouse.yaml.

* The default listen address is 127.0.0.1. Change it to 0.0.0.0 (or the
  interface address) to accept remote connections.

* The ruler must run on one instance only. Configure ruler_storage on
  exactly one lokihouse instance; otherwise alerts and recording-rule
  samples are duplicated.

* Upgrading to 0.9 (schema version 2) or 0.11 (schema version 3) changes the
  ClickHouse schema. Stop every lokihouse instance, run once
    lokihouse -config.file /etc/lokihouse/lokihouse.yaml -config.expand-env -schema.migrate
  (from schema version 1 it needs free disk space for a copy of the largest
  table; from version 2 it only alters the logs table and does not re-index
  existing data), then start
  the service. Without the migration the service exits 1 at start.
  With the password in a systemd credential, run it as a transient unit so
  that CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY is set:
    systemd-run --pty --wait --collect -p ImportCredential='lokihouse.*' \
      /usr/bin/lokihouse -config.file /etc/lokihouse/lokihouse.yaml -config.expand-env -schema.migrate
